AmiNetXDuo, TCP/IP for classic AmigaOS
========================================

AmiNetXDuo is a bsdsocket.library for classic AmigaOS. That shared library
is the one that Amiga network software (browsers, FTP clients, telnet,
file sharing) opens to reach the network. AmiNetXDuo talks to the network
card through the ordinary SANA-II driver of that card. It is configured
with the same files and the same commands as Roadshow.

It is built on Eclipse ThreadX NetX Duo and is MIT licensed throughout.


READ THIS FIRST
---------------

AmiNetXDuo works, and it is not finished.

It gets a DHCP lease, configures itself by SLAAC, answers ARP and
neighbour discovery, pings its gateway, resolves names and moves TCP in
both directions. It scores 142 of 142 on the bsdsocktest conformance
suite. It was tested under emulation on Kickstart 2.04 and 3.1 with a
68000, a 68020 and a 68030, and on a real A3000 with an X-Surf-100.

Classic AmigaOS has no memory protection. A fault in a network stack takes
the machine with it. Save all work before the first attempt.


WHAT IS NEEDED
--------------

  * Any 68000 or better, AmigaOS 2.04 or newer, 1 MB of RAM.
    The archive carries a separate build for the 68000, for the
    68020-68040 and for the 68060, and the Installer picks the right one.
    Encrypted (https:) connections need a 68020 or better.
  * A network card with a SANA-II driver installed in DEVS:
    (a2065.device, ariadne.device, x-surf.device, uaenet.device, ...)
    Drivers are not part of this archive. Use the one for the card.
  * The name of the driver and its unit number, which is usually 0.


INSTALLING
----------

Run the Installer script if there is one. To install by hand:

  copy bsdsocket.library LIBS:
  copy usergroup.library LIBS:
  copy AddNetInterface Online Offline ShowNetStatus netstat ping host TO C:
  makedir DEVS:NetInterfaces
  makedir DEVS:Internet

Do not run two TCP/IP stacks at once. If AmiTCP, Miami or Roadshow is
already installed, rename the old LIBS:bsdsocket.library rather than
overwriting it. Check that the old stack is not started from
S:User-Startup.


SETTING IT UP
-------------

Create one file per network card in DEVS:NetInterfaces. The name of the
file is the name of the interface. For a normal home network with a
router:

  DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0
    DEVICE    = a2065.device
    UNIT      = 0
    CONFIGURE = DHCP

That is the whole configuration. The address, the netmask, the gateway and
the name servers all arrive from the DHCP server.

Note that the address mode keyword is CONFIGURE, not IPTYPE. IPTYPE with a
number after it means something else entirely, the SANA-II packet type,
and is ignored.

Without a DHCP server, use a fixed address instead:

  DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0
    DEVICE    = a2065.device
    UNIT      = 0
    CONFIGURE = STATIC
    ADDRESS   = 192.168.1.10
    NETMASK   = 255.255.255.0

  DEVS:Internet/routes
    DEFAULT = 192.168.1.1

  DEVS:Internet/name_resolution
    nameserver 192.168.1.1

Then start the stack:

  AddNetInterface eth0

Once that works, add this line to S:User-Startup so it happens at every
boot:

  C:AddNetInterface DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0 QUIET

For a server on this machine, a file transfer daemon or a web server, add
it to DEVS:Internet/service_discovery. Other machines on the network then
find it by browsing:

  DEVS:Internet/service_discovery
    _ftp._tcp     21
    _http._tcp    80    Amiga web server

List only a server that is really running. There is a commented example in
the Examples drawer, and the guide has the full description.


IS IT WORKING?
--------------

  ShowNetStatus              is the stack up, and has it got an address?
  ping <the gateway>         can it reach the network?
  host www.aminet.net        do names work?

The first of those to fail is where the problem is. AmiNetXDuo.guide has a
troubleshooting section that covers the usual causes:

  * the wrong device name
  * no DHCP server answering
  * a cable or driver problem
  * a missing interface file
  * a stack that is up while DNS is not

From another machine on the same network, this Amiga also answers to its
own name with .local on the end. "ping amiga.local" works from a Mac, from
Linux with Avahi, and from Windows 10 or later. Nothing has to be
configured for that. It also keeps working when there is no DHCP server to
hand out an address that anyone can guess.


THE COMMANDS
------------

  AddNetInterface   start the stack and bring an interface online
  Online / Offline  put an interface on or off the network
  ShowNetStatus     what the stack thinks the network looks like
  netstat           interfaces, routes and connections
  ping              is that machine there?
  host              look a name up, or an address back

All of them answer "?" with their arguments, in the usual AmigaDOS way.


DOCUMENTATION
-------------

AmiNetXDuo.guide is the manual. Double-click it, or run:

  MultiView AmiNetXDuo.guide

It covers the installation, every configuration file and keyword, the
commands with examples, and troubleshooting.


THINGS IT DOES NOT DO
---------------------

  * At most four interfaces are attached.
  * Only Ethernet-style SANA-II drivers were tested. PPP and SLIP are not
    supported.
  * On a 68020, a first HTTPS handshake to a busy host does not always
    finish inside the timeout of the far end. The next attempt usually
    succeeds, because the session is cached. TLS is off on a 68000.
  * IPv6 multicast group membership is not available to programs.
  * The Roadshow interface-list, routing and statistics library calls
    return ENOSYS.

The guide has the full list with explanations.


